87"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049091} {:db/id 17592186049353} {:db/id 17592186050172}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167264 :question/id #uuid "a8290119-5783-5de1-8f8a-4d7cbbad292c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338488"} {:db/id 17592186167265 :question/id #uuid "97cef292-5a85-5947-928d-d07517a20540" :question/math-academy-id "q-338489"} {:db/id 17592186311951 :question/id #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-338490"} {:db/id 17592186314968 :question/id #uuid "68a9f1f0-e085-5ed7-bf4c-69fbdf0774fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340510"} {:db/id 17592186315068 :question/id #uuid "ea143c38-1de5-5608-b272-c1150223e597" :question/math-academy-id "q-341232"} {:db/id 17592186315069 :question/id #uuid "47637532-5815-59ac-9760-d5eb87953b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-341237"} {:db/id 17592186413394 :question/id #uuid "4d1f9132-9d3c-569e-af11-701e82cf296a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340515"} {:db/id 17592186413395 :question/id #uuid "e155a024-962b-5f82-95d5-406e9c9d0535" :question/math-academy-id "q-340506"} {:db/id 17592186548057 :question/id #uuid "d11bc330-9efc-55d1-a388-de4f864cc23e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340511"}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"} {:db/id 17592186166562 :question/id #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" :question/math-academy-id "e-21787" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/problem "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\nII. $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in[0,\\pi).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=F(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id 17592186049091} {:db/id 17592186049353} {:db/id 17592186050172} {:db/id 17592186167264 :question/id #uuid "a8290119-5783-5de1-8f8a-4d7cbbad292c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338488" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f77c573-2e4c-536e-af1e-09bf69080857" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186164763} {:answer/id #uuid "def9c8ed-19ee-58e5-969f-de9782068ef0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186164764} {:answer/id #uuid "37f67a97-5731-525d-8eea-9e2adde19ccb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186164765} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4cd2e6-0da4-5d24-b3e3-7bda7e4d5751" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186164766} {:answer/id #uuid "7ceba1a1-ec81-5071-86b8-40131cdb7eed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186164767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f77c573-2e4c-536e-af1e-09bf69080857" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186164763} :answer-field/id #uuid "757753c4-07c4-5df7-886c-f4e557b69cad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186164762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "Let $F(x)=2x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+2)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4)=f(1+3)=f(1)=F(1)=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4)=2(4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2)=f(4)=f(1+3)=f(1)=F(1)=2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2(2)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+2)\\ne f(2)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id 17592186167265 :question/id #uuid "97cef292-5a85-5947-928d-d07517a20540" :question/math-academy-id "q-338489"} {:db/id 17592186311951 :question/id #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-338490" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4a08bf6-bbd5-5c7a-a458-cc2f28795f81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450518} {:answer/id #uuid "bc472246-cf39-501b-88ba-aea193a2227d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc472246-cf39-501b-88ba-aea193a2227d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d9ed7ec-92aa-541a-9bea-f3cdc0ae0b0e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186451213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae1de41-bd54-5836-8d70-b2f7e421082c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450520} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9484dd-5f72-59ba-b9bf-a220d37834f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cae1de41-bd54-5836-8d70-b2f7e421082c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450520} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad3fb9a-3bbe-53ce-a61d-00b578edb53b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186451214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfb148a-1391-50e5-a4e7-bcf13b54ab32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450522} {:answer/id #uuid "47fa339f-27e5-5b52-9afc-abbb6a18cfdb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47fa339f-27e5-5b52-9afc-abbb6a18cfdb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450523} :answer-field/id #uuid "187f487c-26a4-5852-ba9e-dad42fa9e85d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186451215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **III.** $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id 17592186314968 :question/id #uuid "68a9f1f0-e085-5ed7-bf4c-69fbdf0774fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340510"} {:db/id 17592186315068 :question/id #uuid "ea143c38-1de5-5608-b272-c1150223e597" :question/math-academy-id "q-341232" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fa4792b-5b15-527a-992e-0d96170ab1aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450530} {:answer/id #uuid "f9461d03-b412-574d-9841-bce08dceb69a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9461d03-b412-574d-9841-bce08dceb69a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450531} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a1e573c-09ca-594f-a9ba-3b2660311b38" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186451219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d83cac8f-f51f-527a-b720-44ad32045870" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450532} {:answer/id #uuid "333f0aac-7943-517c-87c6-fb5685a1c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83cac8f-f51f-527a-b720-44ad32045870" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450532} :answer-field/id #uuid "611505c9-97d8-564c-8e0d-e557fa6b701e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186451220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e9805a-d060-5fce-8210-0537d29aaf51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186450534} {:answer/id #uuid "7605eb1c-ab43-5593-8185-dd779575eaf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7605eb1c-ab43-5593-8185-dd779575eaf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186450535} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ba82cd-be54-5931-a8f3-844d7c5db9bc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186451221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **III.** $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi /2$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Using the periodicity of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, any shift by a multiple of its period returns the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f((x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides equal $f(x),$ the statement is true for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id 17592186315069 :question/id #uuid "47637532-5815-59ac-9760-d5eb87953b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-341237"} {:db/id 17592186413394 :question/id #uuid "4d1f9132-9d3c-569e-af11-701e82cf296a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340515"} {:db/id 17592186413395 :question/id #uuid "e155a024-962b-5f82-95d5-406e9c9d0535" :question/math-academy-id "q-340506"} {:db/id 17592186548057 :question/id #uuid "d11bc330-9efc-55d1-a388-de4f864cc23e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340511" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1091f7e8-6330-5e59-9a2c-9f9973d68732" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186547352} {:answer/id #uuid "316390e2-a9a1-557a-b993-1eb5daa5337e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186547418} {:answer/id #uuid "513baa56-f91e-5c3b-99a2-c83dbfe5f274" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186547500} {:answer/id #uuid "95757a8f-1751-58fa-98e7-c089f0afadab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186547645} {:answer/id #uuid "f962769d-467c-5c55-b93f-dae3d07918c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186547825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f962769d-467c-5c55-b93f-dae3d07918c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186547825} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d9a9a1-d4bf-5f85-97bb-b57a4ffb6644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186547890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "Let $F(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,6)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+12)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,6).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+12)=f((x+6)+6)=f(x+6)=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(7)=f(1+6)=f(1)=F(1)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7)=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=7.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+3)=f(5)=F(5)=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+3)\\ne f(x)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186166563 :question/id #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" :question/math-academy-id "e-21788"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049303} {:db/id 17592186049850} {:db/id 17592186050331}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167274 :question/id #uuid "efd96307-00da-56b8-bab1-563e900c1e1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340366"} {:db/id 17592186167275 :question/id #uuid "beb5a495-580f-541a-b622-3503782168cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340367"} {:db/id 17592186314939 :question/id #uuid "f1b5b0fe-45a4-56a8-8575-f312d7f1dcc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340368"} {:db/id 17592186314940 :question/id #uuid "77cb7a42-6cad-573a-85fb-a6a039017afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340369"} {:db/id 17592186314970 :question/id #uuid "0e763dfc-e659-54e6-9b52-205e19a11f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-340528"} {:db/id 17592186314993 :question/id #uuid "5b9c74cb-227d-5548-a6f8-2b43a9ca07b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-340675"} {:db/id 17592186314994 :question/id #uuid "cc6b217a-5fd4-5ebf-923c-68c576688191" :question/math-academy-id "q-340677"} {:db/id 17592186314995 :question/id #uuid "5b851434-5f95-5831-8b48-c6fb4f555092" :question/math-academy-id "q-340678"} {:db/id 17592186314996 :question/id #uuid "25666762-6caa-548c-81d4-5ffe0a56d215" :question/math-academy-id "q-340681"} {:db/id 17592186315084 :question/id #uuid "22b987d7-9005-5317-ab10-48027150c40e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341340"} {:db/id 17592186397472 :question/id #uuid "3d6ca311-b229-51f5-b6ee-b805697465fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340669"} {:db/id 17592186413393 :question/id #uuid "0c2afbe9-632c-56a2-8877-4a2cf3a61d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-340679"} {:db/id 17592186526085 :question/id #uuid "d85f2141-c945-5a8d-a74b-f86923a41441" :question/math-academy-id "q-340668"}] :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"} {:db/id 17592186166563 :question/id #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" :question/math-academy-id "e-21788" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/problem "$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x)\n=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id 17592186049303} {:db/id 17592186049850} {:db/id 17592186050331} {:db/id 17592186167274 :question/id #uuid "efd96307-00da-56b8-bab1-563e900c1e1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340366" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e324ed91-4fae-5de5-89d5-cd26e5a4b312" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186164863} {:answer/id #uuid "740563d1-b2ae-5c42-94a8-321600325c92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186164864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "740563d1-b2ae-5c42-94a8-321600325c92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186164864} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38d805e-0745-5617-9a20-11b431455d74" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186164862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ddb02b-92cb-56e6-bef2-df860e70ca26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186164867} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca0e3e6-336f-55ab-8f04-9f67ade95df9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186164868} {:answer/id #uuid "2f604df7-206c-51f7-9878-ccf3987ff08a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186164869} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f44a38-9c4d-555c-8e31-fc73981a0d16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186547816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca0e3e6-336f-55ab-8f04-9f67ade95df9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186164868} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace87305-ddfd-54fb-a584-0e03b68cf02b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186164865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id 17592186167275 :question/id #uuid "beb5a495-580f-541a-b622-3503782168cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340367" :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43785bed-6354-5520-9544-515eb3f47955" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312514} {:answer/id #uuid "73a37284-23f3-5418-992b-555b44531652" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312891} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a906103-9969-5dec-8e2d-71d03c4cc319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2534ce9c-b049-551d-a7e3-68e08b499839" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186312258} {:answer/id #uuid "2eba7fa0-cdf8-5b6c-84f5-6252bbc05561" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id 17592186312345} {:answer/id #uuid "363665b7-fab1-5111-805b-48107890efd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186312412} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecab699-9c2c-5d02-8af2-cf07ae896cef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186312481} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2d662-c721-53c0-ac55-ccfaedc98bf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186313048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312481} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5b36bc-ff84-53e3-b504-b0efcc87ced7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id 17592186314939 :question/id #uuid "f1b5b0fe-45a4-56a8-8575-f312d7f1dcc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340368"} {:db/id 17592186314940 :question/id #uuid "77cb7a42-6cad-573a-85fb-a6a039017afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340369"} {:db/id 17592186314970 :question/id #uuid "0e763dfc-e659-