swer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id 17592186413370} {:answer/id #uuid "de6a0d2c-a56d-5bb1-9b1c-cd4e2312cdc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186413376} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd7dac4-b5f5-51c0-9495-3c31b7bdebf2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/id 17592186413384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id 17592186413356} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe88487e-f897-504c-b0b7-1ea54245c515" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186413392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+9x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186314985 :question/id #uuid "189f6686-0cc6-5c02-8e21-1097f02fd5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-340628"} {:db/id 17592186314987 :question/id #uuid "0dea1a3b-78f2-5704-9965-e3fb9de63bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340647" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fed8a6-dde9-59a6-bc31-184c4876910c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :d