orrect {:answer/id #uuid "a485b2b2-9921-5fd5-bd9d-f8899940f0fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186313268} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a717f2-4ea4-5657-bb93-c452e09b74ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186314202} {:answer/id #uuid "a485b2b2-9921-5fd5-bd9d-f8899940f0fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186313268} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2136e8-d9d6-5af6-a549-5e509b077f3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186313579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b12c1a-93ae-5bc6-9fe8-42c8eda21ee1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186312119} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4f5c80-0a03-57e7-8507-5198d48c6bfc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186312177} {:answer/id #uuid "4693d13b-e7f3-533f-a819-ee280e597461" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186312552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7359a2-3aa5-59d3-a824-65f7cf6dc11f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186313001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b12c1a-93ae-5bc6-9fe8-42c8eda21ee1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186312119} :answer-field/id #uuid "98de6f2f-ef74-5be0-a949-95ef2c54203f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186314426} {:answer/id #uuid "14b12c1a-93ae-5bc6-9fe8-42c8eda21ee1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186312119} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4f5c80-0a03-57e7-8507-5198d48c6bfc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186312177} {:answer/id #uuid "4693d13b-e7f3-533f-a819-ee280e597461" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186312552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7359a2-3aa5-59d3-a824-65f7cf6dc11f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186313001} {:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186167943} :activity/id #uuid "a585794a-f4e3-5afb-9bdf-f3bee0610206" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049306 :topic/id #uuid "eb34afb7-9644-525e-b42b-cf82816ee5e0" :topic/math-academy-id 3195} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186167943 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168034 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will represent a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ as an infinite sum of simple sine and cosine waves. This representation is called the **Fourier series** for $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nThe key idea is that we can \"build\" a complicated periodic graph by adding together basic waves. For example, below we have a discontinuous periodic function approximated by the first few terms of its Fourier series.\n\n![](images/39/392a8df0fab00010e76036aa0fec2c60f25b5766b3cacc7983b7443d909866eb.png)\n\nThis gives us a powerful way to analyze periodic behavior by breaking it down into simple oscillations. Fourier series play a central role in modeling vibrations, signals, and other repeating patterns.\n\nTo work with Fourier series, we typically start with a function defined on a single base interval, such as $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ and then extend it to be periodic over all real numbers. This process creates what is called a **periodic extension**.\n\nIn the next slide, we will define this concept formally and look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d19e02d-99c0-5649-b7a3-bdb37c8e97e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "822d573c-a967-52e4-854c-d2b5f14b0fff" :step/math-academy-id 600573 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167955}} {:db/id 17592186167944 :step/content {:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186166562 :question/id #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" :question/math-academy-id "e-21787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049091} {:db/id 17592186049353} {:db/id 17592186050172}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167264} {:db/id 17592186167265} {:db/id 17592186311951} {:db/id 17592186314968} {:db/id 17592186315068} {:db/id 17592186315069} {:db/id 17592186413394} {:db/id 17592186413395} {:db/id 17592186548057}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c6f0ea8f-2008-5f49-84d9-7341157766fc" :step/math-academy-id 600574 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167945}} {:db/id 17592186167945 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168036 :tutorial/content "Recall that we can define an inner product on functions over $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAlso recall that functions $f$ and $g$ are called *orthogonal* (with respect to this inner product) if $\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=0.$\n\nThe key fact we will use throughout this lesson is that the functions\n\n$$\n1,\\ \\cos(x),\\ \\sin(x),\\ \\cos(2x),\\ \\sin(2x),\\ \\ldots\n$$\n\nare orthogonal with respect to the inner product defined above.\n\nMore precisely, for integers $m,n\\ge 1,$ we have the following orthogonality relations on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n- $\\langle \\cos(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$ and\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $m,n.$\n\nAlso, the constant function $1$ is orthogonal to every sine and cosine:\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ and\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nFinally, we will also use the squared norms of these functions:\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,1\\rangle=2\\pi,\n\\quad\n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\pi\n\\quad\n\\text{for all } n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in concrete examples.\n\n**Note:** Both intervals $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ and $[0,2\\pi)$ are considered standard in this context. We’ll use $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ in tutorials, but some exercises use $[0,2\\pi).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9c1c7f37-624c-5c49-9ded-2b3dd3b37d7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14639 :tutorial/title "Reminder: Orthogonal Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f80997bd-d9ed-5a35-b485-c34868575480" :step/math-academy-id 600578 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167946}} {:db/id 17592186167946 :step/content {:db/id 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186166563 :question/id #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" :question/math-academy-id "e-21788"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049303} {:db/id 17592186049850} {:db/id 17592186050331}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167274} {:db/id 17592186167275} {:db/id 17592186314939} {:db/id 17592186314940} {:db/id 17592186314970} {:db/id 17592186314993} {:db/id 17592186314994} {:db/id 17592186314995} {:db/id 17592186314996} {:db/id 17592186315084} {:db/id 17592186397472} {:db/id 17592186413393} {:db/id 17592186526085}] :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "b4a0ba44-f514-5920-a47f-39a8f107bb7d" :step/math-academy-id 600579 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167947}} {:db/id 17592186167947 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168035 :tutorial/content "Recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We can represent $f(x)$ by its Fourier series:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the formulas for the coefficients $a_0, a_n,$ and $b_n.$\n\nTo do this, we'll use a powerful idea from linear algebra. We define an *inner product* for functions on the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ as\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWith this inner product, the set of functions $\\{1, \\cos(nx), \\sin(nx)\\}$ for $n\\ge 1$ is an *orthogonal set*.\n\nThis means we can think of the Fourier coefficients as *projections* of the function $f(x)$ onto each of these orthogonal \"directions\". The coefficients $a_0,a_n,b_n$ simply tell us \"how much\" of $f(x)$ lies in each of these directions.\n\nOn the next slide, we will use this idea to derive the formula for the first coefficient, $a_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "304c879d-2d08-5f1e-985b-938b67e64dfb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14628 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Form For 2pi-Periodic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "769e9784-6852-5663-879d-f2dd13159e0a" :step/math-academy-id 600583 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167956}} {:db/id 17592186167948 :step/content {:db/id 17592186166964 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186166564 :question/id #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21789"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048169} {:db/id 17592186050841}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167282} {:db/id 17592186167291} {:db/id 17592186314985} {:db/id 17592186314987} {:db/id 17592186314988} {:db/id 17592186314989} {:db/id 17592186314991} {:db/id 17592186315082} {:db/id 17592186397418} {:db/id 17592186397438} {:db/id 17592186397539} {:db/id 17592186547996} {:db/id 17592186548063}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "52c506ad-0e1c-5f71-b513-284e3650dd10" :step/math-academy-id 600584 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167949}} {:db/id 17592186167949 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168043 :tutorial/content "Once again, recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We represent $f$ by its Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients, we use the inner product on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe have already seen the derivation for the coefficient $a_0$ early in the lesson.\n\nNow, we derive the formula for $a_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\cos(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\cos(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we derive the formula for $b_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\sin(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc7b8526-b305-5026-8e7d-5e426bbe1a15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14668 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formulas For the Fourier Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "4fc54380-13dd-5632-a907-a555a9ed5733" :step/math-academy-id 600587} {:db/id 17592186167955 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168052 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *$p$-periodic* if $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$  \n\nNow, if $F(x)$ is defined on an interval $x \\in [a,b)$ of length $p,$ then its **$p$-periodic extension** $f$ is defined by the two properties:  \n\n- $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b).$\n\nFor example, let $F(x) = -x$ be defined on $x\\in [0,2).$ Now, suppose $f(x)$ is the $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe66db8a73acee21bbd5f7c95d7598b448cb678408d2f47754ee4c30aefd5b14.png)\n\n**Watch out!** We should always be aware of the difference between $F(x)$ and its extension $f(x).$ For instance, consider the function $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ defined on $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ and its $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x).$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1299bb593554a930a12870d39c74aa8d23ea97027b9e9e253e8fda6b592e42a.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not just $2\\sin(x)!$ But it would coincide with $2\\sin(x)$ if we were to consider the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ from, say, $x \\in [0,2\\pi]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbf1ed42-199b-56dd-ba70-e35c3597339a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14853 :tutorial/title "Periodic Extensions"} :step/id #uuid "c98bd153-6ef6-5953-a668-095e59197be9" :step/math-academy-id 606168 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167944}} {:db/id 17592186167956 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168053 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the formula for the coefficient $a_0$ by taking the inner product of the entire Fourier series \n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nwith the constant function $1.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Start with the Fourier series and take the inner product of both sides with $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big),\\,1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Use the linearity of the inner product to distribute it across the sum:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Apply the orthogonality property. For any $n\\ge 1,$ we have $\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle=0$ and $\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle=0.$ This makes all the terms in the sums equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Solve for $a_0.$ We just need to calculate $\\langle 1,1\\rangle=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} 1\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x=2\\pi.$\n\n$$\na_0=\\dfrac{2\\langle f(x),1\\rangle}{\\langle 1,1\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{2\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x}{2\\pi}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe find the formulas for $a_n$ and $b_n$ (for $n\\ge 1$) in a similar way.\n\n- To find $a_n,$ we take the inner product of the Fourier series with $\\cos(nx).$ By orthogonality, the only term that is not zero on the right-hand side is the one involving $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = a_n \\langle \\cos(nx), \\cos(nx)\\rangle\n$$\n\nSince $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\pi,$ we can solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n- Similarly, to find $b_n,$ we take the inner product with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n**Note:** If $f$ is the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of a function $F$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ then we can replace $f(x)$ with $F(x)$ inside the integrals.\n\nNow that we have the formulas for all the coefficients, let's work through a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5d2770f-53de-5939-9ec7-4155aef67173" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14854 :tutorial/title "The Formula For the Constant Term"} :step/id #uuid "a0bab88b-45ac-5613-b8cf-64b1eafabee1" :step/math-academy-id 606214 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167948}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186166990} {:db/id 17592186167943 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168034 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will represent a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ as an infinite sum of simple sine and cosine waves. This representation is called the **Fourier series** for $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nThe key idea is that we can \"build\" a complicated periodic graph by adding together basic waves. For example, below we have a discontinuous periodic function approximated by the first few terms of its Fourier series.\n\n![](images/39/392a8df0fab00010e76036aa0fec2c60f25b5766b3cacc7983b7443d909866eb.png)\n\nThis gives us a powerful way to analyze periodic behavior by breaking it down into simple oscillations. Fourier series play a central role in modeling vibrations, signals, and other repeating patterns.\n\nTo work with Fourier series, we typically start with a function defined on a single base interval, such as $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ and then extend it to be periodic over all real numbers. This process creates what is called a **periodic extension**.\n\nIn the next slide, we will define this concept formally and look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d19e02d-99c0-5649-b7a3-bdb37c8e97e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "822d573c-a967-52e4-854c-d2b5f14b0fff" :step/math-academy-id 600573 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167955}} {:db/id 17592186168034 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will represent a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ as an infinite sum of simple sine and cosine waves. This representation is called the **Fourier series** for $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nThe key idea is that we can \"build\" a complicated periodic graph by adding together basic waves. For example, below we have a discontinuous periodic function approximated by the first few terms of its Fourier series.\n\n![](images/39/392a8df0fab00010e76036aa0fec2c60f25b5766b3cacc7983b7443d909866eb.png)\n\nThis gives us a powerful way to analyze periodic behavior by breaking it down into simple oscillations. Fourier series play a central role in modeling vibrations, signals, and other repeating patterns.\n\nTo work with Fourier series, we typically start with a function defined on a single base interval, such as $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ and then extend it to be periodic over all real numbers. This process creates what is called a **periodic extension**.\n\nIn the next slide, we will define this concept formally and look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d19e02d-99c0-5649-b7a3-bdb37c8e97e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186167955 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168052 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *$p$-periodic* if $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$  \n\nNow, if $F(x)$ is defined on an interval $x \\in [a,b)$ of length $p,$ then its **$p$-periodic extension** $f$ is defined by the two properties:  \n\n- $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b).$\n\nFor example, let $F(x) = -x$ be defined on $x\\in [0,2).$ Now, suppose $f(x)$ is the $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe66db8a73acee21bbd5f7c95d7598b448cb678408d2f47754ee4c30aefd5b14.png)\n\n**Watch out!** We should always be aware of the difference between $F(x)$ and its extension $f(x).$ For instance, consider the function $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ defined on $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ and its $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x).$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1299bb593554a930a12870d39c74aa8d23ea97027b9e9e253e8fda6b592e42a.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not just $2\\sin(x)!$ But it would coincide with $2\\sin(x)$ if we were to consider the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ from, say, $x \\in [0,2\\pi]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbf1ed42-199b-56dd-ba70-e35c3597339a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14853 :tutorial/title "Periodic Extensions"} :step/id #uuid "c98bd153-6ef6-5953-a668-095e59197be9" :step/math-academy-id 606168 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167944}} {:db/id 17592186167944 :step/content {:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186166562 :question/id #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" :question/math-academy-id "e-21787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049091} {:db/id 17592186049353} {:db/id 17592186050172}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167264} {:db/id 17592186167265} {:db/id 17592186311951} {:db/id 17592186314968} {:db/id 17592186315068} {:db/id 17592186315069} {:db/id 17592186413394} {:db/id 17592186413395} {:db/id 17592186548057}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c6f0ea8f-2008-5f49-84d9-7341157766fc" :step/math-academy-id 600574 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167945}} {:db/id 17592186167945 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168036 :tutorial/content "Recall that we can define an inner product on functions over $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAlso recall that functions $f$ and $g$ are called *orthogonal* (with respect to this inner product) if $\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=0.$\n\nThe key fact we will use throughout this lesson is that the functions\n\n$$\n1,\\ \\cos(x),\\ \\sin(x),\\ \\cos(2x),\\ \\sin(2x),\\ \\ldots\n$$\n\nare orthogonal with respect to the inner product defined above.\n\nMore precisely, for integers $m,n\\ge 1,$ we have the following orthogonality relations on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n- $\\langle \\cos(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$ and\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $m,n.$\n\nAlso, the constant function $1$ is orthogonal to every sine and cosine:\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ and\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nFinally, we will also use the squared norms of these functions:\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,1\\rangle=2\\pi,\n\\quad\n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\pi\n\\quad\n\\text{for all } n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in concrete examples.\n\n**Note:** Both intervals $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ and $[0,2\\pi)$ are considered standard in this context. We’ll use $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ in tutorials, but some exercises use $[0,2\\pi).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9c1c7f37-624c-5c49-9ded-2b3dd3b37d7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14639 :tutorial/title "Reminder: Orthogonal Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f80997bd-d9ed-5a35-b485-c34868575480" :step/math-academy-id 600578 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167946}} {:db/id 17592186168036 :tutorial/content "Recall that we can define an inner product on functions over $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAlso recall that functions $f$ and $g$ are called *orthogonal* (with respect to this inner product) if $\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=0.$\n\nThe key fact we will use throughout this lesson is that the functions\n\n$$\n1,\\ \\cos(x),\\ \\sin(x),\\ \\cos(2x),\\ \\sin(2x),\\ \\ldots\n$$\n\nare orthogonal with respect to the inner product defined above.\n\nMore precisely, for integers $m,n\\ge 1,$ we have the following orthogonality relations on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n- $\\langle \\cos(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$ and\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $m,n.$\n\nAlso, the constant function $1$ is orthogonal to every sine and cosine:\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ and\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nFinally, we will also use the squared norms of these functions:\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,1\\rangle=2\\pi,\n\\quad\n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\pi\n\\quad\n\\text{for all } n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in concrete examples.\n\n**Note:** Both intervals $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ and $[0,2\\pi)$ are considered standard in this context. We’ll use $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ in tutorials, but some exercises use $[0,2\\pi).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9c1c7f37-624c-5c49-9ded-2b3dd3b37d7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14639 :tutorial/title "Reminder: Orthogonal Functions"} {:db/id 17592186167946 :step/content {:db/id 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186166563 :question/id #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" :question/math-academy-id "e-21788"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049303} {:db/id 17592186049850} {:db/id 17592186050331}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167274} {:db/id 17592186167275} {:db/id 17592186314939} {:db/id 17592186314940} {:db/id 17592186314970} {:db/id 17592186314993} {:db/id 17592186314994} {:db/id 17592186314995} {:db/id 17592186314996} {:db/id 17592186315084} {:db/id 17592186397472} {:db/id 17592186413393} {:db/id 17592186526085}] :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "b4a0ba44-f514-5920-a47f-39a8f107bb7d" :step/math-academy-id 600579 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167947}} {:db/id 17592186167947 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168035 :tutorial/content "Recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We can represent $f(x)$ by its Fourier series:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the formulas for the coefficients $a_0, a_n,$ and $b_n.$\n\nTo do this, we'll use a powerful idea from linear algebra. We define an *inner product* for functions on the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ as\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWith this inner product, the set of functions $\\{1, \\cos(nx), \\sin(nx)\\}$ for $n\\ge 1$ is an *orthogonal set*.\n\nThis means we can think of the Fourier coefficients as *projections* of the function $f(x)$ onto each of these orthogonal \"directions\". The coefficients $a_0,a_n,b_n$ simply tell us \"how much\" of $f(x)$ lies in each of these directions.\n\nOn the next slide, we will use this idea to derive the formula for the first coefficient, $a_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "304c879d-2d08-5f1e-985b-938b67e64dfb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14628 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Form For 2pi-Periodic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "769e9784-6852-5663-879d-f2dd13159e0a" :step/math-academy-id 600583 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167956}} {:db/id 17592186168035 :tutorial/content "Recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We can represent $f(x)$ by its Fourier series:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the formulas for the coefficients $a_0, a_n,$ and $b_n.$\n\nTo do this, we'll use a powerful idea from linear algebra. We define an *inner product* for functions on the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ as\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWith this inner product, the set of functions $\\{1, \\cos(nx), \\sin(nx)\\}$ for $n\\ge 1$ is an *orthogonal set*.\n\nThis means we can think of the Fourier coefficients as *projections* of the function $f(x)$ onto each of these orthogonal \"directions\". The coefficients $a_0,a_n,b_n$ simply tell us \"how much\" of $f(x)$ lies in each of these directions.\n\nOn the next slide, we will use this idea to derive the formula for the first coefficient, $a_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "304c879d-2d08-5f1e-985b-938b67e64dfb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14628 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Form For 2pi-Periodic Functions"} {:db/id 17592186167956 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168053 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the formula for the coefficient $a_0$ by taking the inner product of the entire Fourier series \n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nwith the constant function $1.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Start with the Fourier series and take the inner product of both sides with $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big),\\,1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Use the linearity of the inner product to distribute it across the sum:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Apply the orthogonality property. For any $n\\ge 1,$ we have $\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle=0$ and $\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle=0.$ This makes all the terms in the sums equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Solve for $a_0.$ We just need to calculate $\\langle 1,1\\rangle=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} 1\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x=2\\pi.$\n\n$$\na_0=\\dfrac{2\\langle f(x),1\\rangle}{\\langle 1,1\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{2\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x}{2\\pi}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe find the formulas for $a_n$ and $b_n$ (for $n\\ge 1$) in a similar way.\n\n- To find $a_n,$ we take the inner product of the Fourier series with $\\cos(nx).$ By orthogonality, the only term that is not zero on the right-hand side is the one involving $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = a_n \\langle \\cos(nx), \\cos(nx)\\rangle\n$$\n\nSince $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\pi,$ we can solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n- Similarly, to find $b_n,$ we take the inner product with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n**Note:** If $f$ is the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of a function $F$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ then we can replace $f(x)$ with $F(x)$ inside the integrals.\n\nNow that we have the formulas for all the coefficients, let's work through a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5d2770f-53de-5939-9ec7-4155aef67173" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14854 :tutorial/title "The Formula For the Constant Term"} :step/id #uuid "a0bab88b-45ac-5613-b8cf-64b1eafabee1" :step/math-academy-id 606214 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167948}} {:db/id 17592186167948 :step/content {:db/id 17592186166964 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186166564 :question/id #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21789"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048169} {:db/id 17592186050841}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186167282} {:db/id 17592186167291} {:db/id 17592186314985} {:db/id 17592186314987} {:db/id 17592186314988} {:db/id 17592186314989} {:db/id 17592186314991} {:db/id 17592186315082} {:db/id 17592186397418} {:db/id 17592186397438} {:db/id 17592186397539} {:db/id 17592186547996} {:db/id 17592186548063}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "52c506ad-0e1c-5f71-b513-284e3650dd10" :step/math-academy-id 600584 :step/next {:db/id 17592186167949}} {:db/id 17592186167949 :step/content {:db/id 17592186168043 :tutorial/content "Once again, recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We represent $f$ by its Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients, we use the inner product on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe have already seen the derivation for the coefficient $a_0$ early in the lesson.\n\nNow, we derive the formula for $a_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\cos(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\cos(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we derive the formula for $b_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\sin(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc7b8526-b305-5026-8e7d-5e426bbe1a15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14668 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formulas For the Fourier Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "4fc54380-13dd-5632-a907-a555a9ed5733" :step/math-academy-id 600587} {:db/id 17592186168043 :tutorial/content "Once again, recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We represent $f$ by its Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients, we use the inner product on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe have already seen the derivation for the coefficient $a_0$ early in the lesson.\n\nNow, we derive the formula for $a_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\cos(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\cos(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we derive the formula for $b_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\sin(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc7b8526-b305-5026-8e7d-5e426bbe1a15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14668 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formulas For the Fourier Coefficients"} {:db/id 17592186168052 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *$p$-periodic* if $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$  \n\nNow, if $F(x)$ is defined on an interval $x \\in [a,b)$ of length $p,$ then its **$p$-periodic extension** $f$ is defined by the two properties:  \n\n- $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b).$\n\nFor example, let $F(x) = -x$ be defined on $x\\in [0,2).$ Now, suppose $f(x)$ is the $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe66db8a73acee21bbd5f7c95d7598b448cb678408d2f47754ee4c30aefd5b14.png)\n\n**Watch out!** We should always be aware of the difference between $F(x)$ and its extension $f(x).$ For instance, consider the function $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ defined on $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ and its $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x).$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1299bb593554a930a12870d39c74aa8d23ea97027b9e9e253e8fda6b592e42a.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not just $2\\sin(x)!$ But it would coincide with $2\\sin(x)$ if we were to consider the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ from, say, $x \\in [0,2\\pi]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbf1ed42-199b-56dd-ba70-e35c3597339a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14853 :tutorial/title "Periodic Extensions"} {:db/id 17592186168053 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the formula for the coefficient $a_0$ by taking the inner product of the entire Fourier series \n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nwith the constant function $1.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Start with the Fourier series and take the inner product of both sides with $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big),\\,1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Use the linearity of the inner product to distribute it across the sum:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Apply the orthogonality property. For any $n\\ge 1,$ we have $\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle=0$ and $\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle=0.$ This makes all the terms in the sums equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Solve for $a_0.$ We just need to calculate $\\langle 1,1\\rangle=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} 1\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x=2\\pi.$\n\n$$\na_0=\\dfrac{2\\langle f(x),1\\rangle}{\\langle 1,1\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{2\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x}{2\\pi}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe find the formulas for $a_n$ and $b_n$ (for $n\\ge 1$) in a similar way.\n\n- To find $a_n,$ we take the inner product of the Fourier series with $\\cos(nx).$ By orthogonality, the only term that is not zero on the right-hand side is the one involving $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = a_n \\langle \\cos(nx), \\cos(nx)\\rangle\n$$\n\nSince $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\pi,$ we can solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n- Similarly, to find $b_n,$ we take the inner product with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n**Note:** If $f$ is the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of a function $F$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ then we can replace $f(x)$ with $F(x)$ inside the integrals.\n\nNow that we have the formulas for all the coefficients, let's work through a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5d2770f-53de-5939-9ec7-4155aef67173" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14854 :tutorial/title "The Formula For the Constant Term"} {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} {:activity/course {:db/id 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186526109} :activity/id #uuid "5a7d7bdb-8d3b-55d5-bde7-c86a8c028831" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049306 :topic/id #uuid "eb34afb7-9644-525e-b42b-cf82816ee5e0" :topic/math-academy-id 3195} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186526109 :step/content {:db/id 17592186167275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43785bed-6354-5520-9544-515eb3f47955" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312514} {:answer/id #uuid "73a37284-23f3-5418-992b-555b44531652" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312891} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a906103-9969-5dec-8e2d-71d03c4cc319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2534ce9c-b049-551d-a7e3-68e08b499839" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186312258} {:answer/id #uuid "2eba7fa0-cdf8-5b6c-84f5-6252bbc05561" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id 17592186312345} {:answer/id #uuid "363665b7-fab1-5111-805b-48107890efd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186312412} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecab699-9c2c-5d02-8af2-cf07ae896cef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186312481} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2d662-c721-53c0-ac55-ccfaedc98bf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186313048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312481} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5b36bc-ff84-53e3-b504-b0efcc87ced7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "beb5a495-580f-541a-b622-3503782168cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340367" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} :step/id #uuid "0b9bb9a4-b5ba-5bfc-bc1c-4533a92d6e51" :step/next {:db/id 17592186526126}} {:db/id 17592186526126 :step/content {:db/id 17592186314987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fed8a6-dde9-59a6-bc31-184c4876910c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186312040} {:answer/id #uuid "27e0630f-6397-5f50-acaf-792c1ed4a63f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186312279} {:answer/id #uuid "43aed6aa-dcf1-5ad7-a81d-0980770c21f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186312518} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4f1e1b-19ff-53ba-846a-bf53f48da618" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186312998} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b040b0-f184-56be-ac1d-d88fac453333" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313510} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8ab6ed-8ef1-52e1-ac24-6d360702fe0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186313879} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dec56fd-2f38-5aed-ad79-60665513c886" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e31a26-dc64-56ae-8b02-5df0ca29f692" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186312253} {:answer/id #uuid "5e61143e-df6c-5b8e-8136-4496a9d8064e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186312731} {:answer/id #uuid "76e1a4a5-6452-5ca7-a013-0a1ec3006b61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186312925} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa3c86f-7425-56ba-ad81-e457cf095af9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186313077} {:answer/id #uuid "b44fa11f-bd26-5369-aa04-d7d89af929b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186313376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186313077} :answer-field/id #uuid "5590368c-eeac-509d-bc7c-56a5a5ab8a99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02879327-9074-58b7-a673-9af647dd76a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186311978} {:answer/id #uuid "2e41812a-03d8-5475-8f34-ec05ee42075a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186312338} {:answer/id #uuid "37bacd25-c8a9-5643-a0a5-6efe9349da92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186312423} {:answer/id #uuid "c9929a36-9c78-5386-8613-645d48af54de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186313547} {:answer/id #uuid "f8854e4c-1476-5259-80df-34a5b76b1f6b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id 17592186313960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312338} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa2110c0-5101-54d7-887b-aa21835a6e99" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0dea1a3b-78f2-5704-9965-e3fb9de63bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340647" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{2}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7db2bec7-56bf-57ee-b02e-da70da5a3acc" :step/next {:db/id 17592186526130}} {:db/id 17592186526130 :step/content {:db/id 17592186314991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c52c11-2a0f-58d6-aff5-fbcff80b806b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186312230} {:answer/id #uuid "286e0c18-97d0-532c-bf41-6751830ae151" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186312282} {:answer/id #uuid "a350f6d6-24d8-5f63-b205-477f47e6d19e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186313260} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b2f783-0c8d-5f89-9d7f-07e8c45faf2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186313411} {:answer/id #uuid "e477c6c9-364e-5141-949d-9701044a0b0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186313778}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312230} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc086cc-8293-54fb-ab53-553c0b9185e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6adb5300-558b-5171-ae85-ccd08169caac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186312814} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4de8-2de1-5c8d-aa10-e8dc5e05701b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313040} {:answer/id #uuid "b6112b1f-27d0-5ea9-9414-69665412c45e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313389} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d80ee5-4b61-5649-991b-08f557a8dc02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313493} {:answer/id #uuid "ea34fcb0-83d8-5c60-9aab-be6de2adf3d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313839} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ab1a37-3aeb-50e5-ac0c-f636df7e8865" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312814} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3676550-2291-58a0-8716-0b0471438972" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21099cf0-6ade-5176-97e2-98b51987b126" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id 17592186312226} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9b48c8-5e3a-5bc2-a5ab-b517fd3e8a59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186312487} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73d4a2-4ad0-5837-b408-af5ab8f8dfdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186312737} {:answer/id #uuid "b835c93d-0f3b-59f6-8a79-699819da02b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id 17592186313406} {:answer/id #uuid "f35dc895-fc13-50f6-aa56-710cb0aafcfb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id 17592186313922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312737} :answer-field/id #uuid "f825e814-46db-5a49-9ae3-db2a2ad9a646" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc60bc57-5b9c-5f88-9bc5-5ecffd1981e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340662" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d83434b-1efb-5984-8cbe-1830c4537f9d" :step/next {:db/id 17592186526142}} {:db/id 17592186526142 :step/content {:db/id 17592186526085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b761040-6bc5-5857-9c79-db2a13d27611" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186525349} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa6194-fdee-5a52-85e0-2ed9f495bf4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186525797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186525797} :answer-field/id #uuid "55543445-1727-5019-a16d-94fb1228f51e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186525890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5912f1-7503-5114-bd94-c09318d2aa64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186525231} {:answer/id #uuid "bf39a84a-5b39-5bb5-8f67-5b65a043baf3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186525695} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84a8fc-5d46-52dc-beb9-dfab6973e8cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186525796} {:answer/id #uuid "fea342fe-4c4d-5c10-87ec-9f65ac0a4202" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186525840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186525840} :answer-field/id #uuid "f22a4739-c628-52a9-ae16-699a277e6c24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186525971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d85f2141-c945-5a8d-a74b-f86923a41441" :question/math-academy-id "q-340668" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} :step/id #uuid "c775e9dd-c091-5db8-8cc4-86b9ae65888c" :step/next {:db/id 17592186526146}} {:db/id 17592186526146 :step/content {:db/id 17592186315082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067f1f01-946a-508e-8cd3-212922a30cb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186312018} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9ff7e5-36fe-5003-9799-45faaef030fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id 17592186312189} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6042f8-3d6d-5360-9b00-ffce5586d196" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186312954} {:answer/id #uuid "9d043cac-3ffa-5f03-90e2-e0c96f5392e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186313218} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d8c3cb-4ca0-5236-8d59-7a3088019974" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186313774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186313218} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c8bc7b-4cd5-5227-9be1-4358ff24b62e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ffdb817-1342-5108-9142-29c41b65bfb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186312744} {:answer/id #uuid "65761c3a-2090-56c5-acc5-a7f5ae659917" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186312774} {:answer/id #uuid "821891c1-b858-5bda-9626-34240d7900c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313023} {:answer/id #uuid "832cde4d-8aee-5316-8503-fd0c2df797ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313031} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ae89cc-5e23-5a8d-aa64-bd8dbd7a3313" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186313912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186313912} :answer-field/id #uuid "82f86a36-0a17-52e0-b0d0-d33c3e50f91e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec31f7d-80af-523d-b38f-294b8ad028bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/id 17592186312081} {:answer/id #uuid "0f14e142-4150-51d0-8ede-ebf4f1bd28f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186312085} {:answer/id #uuid "114b3cdc-ae09-538f-94d9-f5c3e4392e2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id 17592186312098} {:answer/id #uuid "992303bc-e624-5324-b23d-d78df9b645e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id 17592186313189} {:answer/id #uuid "dd23267e-19c8-5ea7-b897-d3089e3d52ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id 17592186313714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312098} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b3226d-31b8-5280-8683-73d653641be2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a5448f9e-c9df-59b5-abdc-33e558c56456" :question/math-academy-id "q-341309" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e117ceb3-8a58-58f9-8d3a-2b451769283b"}] :activity/title "Introduction to Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id 17592186525200} {:db/id 17592186046644} {:db/id 17592186526109 :step/content {:db/id 17592186167275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43785bed-6354-5520-9544-515eb3f47955" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312514} {:answer/id #uuid "73a37284-23f3-5418-992b-555b44531652" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312891} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a906103-9969-5dec-8e2d-71d03c4cc319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2534ce9c-b049-551d-a7e3-68e08b499839" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186312258} {:answer/id #uuid "2eba7fa0-cdf8-5b6c-84f5-6252bbc05561" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id 17592186312345} {:answer/id #uuid "363665b7-fab1-5111-805b-48107890efd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186312412} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecab699-9c2c-5d02-8af2-cf07ae896cef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186312481} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2d662-c721-53c0-ac55-ccfaedc98bf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186313048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312481} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5b36bc-ff84-53e3-b504-b0efcc87ced7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "beb5a495-580f-541a-b622-3503782168cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340367" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} :step/id #uuid "0b9bb9a4-b5ba-5bfc-bc1c-4533a92d6e51" :step/next {:db/id 17592186526126}} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43785bed-6354-5520-9544-515eb3f47955" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312514} {:answer/id #uuid "73a37284-23f3-5418-992b-555b44531652" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312891} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a906103-9969-5dec-8e2d-71d03c4cc319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314040} {:answer/id #uuid "43785bed-6354-5520-9544-515eb3f47955" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312514} {:answer/id #uuid "73a37284-23f3-5418-992b-555b44531652" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id 17592186312891} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2534ce9c-b049-551d-a7e3-68e08b499839" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186312258} {:answer/id #uuid "2eba7fa0-cdf8-5b6c-84f5-6252bbc05561" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id 17592186312345} {:answer/id #uuid "363665b7-fab1-5111-805b-48107890efd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186312412} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecab699-9c2c-5d02-8af2-cf07ae896cef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186312481} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2d662-c721-53c0-ac55-ccfaedc98bf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186313048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186312481} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5b36bc-ff84-53e3-b504-b0efcc87ced7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494} :db/id 17592186314533} 